===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=limit|:inh|enm:terme<ety:bor<fro:terme<ety:inh<la:terminus>>>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|terme}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|terme}}, from {{der|en|la|terminus|t=a bound, boundary, limit, end; in {{cog|la-med|-}}, also a time, period, word, covenant, etc.}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*térmn̥||stump, end, boundary}}.

{{doublet|en|terminus|termon}}. {{cog|ang|-}} had {{m|ang|termen}}, from the same source.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|limit or terminus, broadly}} {{lb|en|original meaning, by derivation}} That which limits the extent of anything; limit, extremity, bound, boundary, {{l|en|terminus#Noun}}.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=Corruption is a reciprocal to generation, and they two are as nature's two '''terms''', or boundaries.}}
#* {{RQ:Shelley Poetical Works|I|The Revolt of Islam|268|At the decline of day,<br>Winding above the mountain’s snowy '''term''',<br>New banners shone: {{...}}}}
# A condition or restriction.
## {{senseid|en|condition, legally, in general}} {{lb|en|often in plural}} Any of the [[binding]] [[condition]]s or [[promise]]s in a  [[contract]] or [[agreement]].
##: {{ux|en|Be sure to read the '''terms''' and conditions before signing.}}
##: {{ux|en|Alright, look...we can spend the holidays with your parents, but this time it will be on my '''terms'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|condition, legally, regarding payment}} {{lb|en|in plural}} Specifically, the [[condition]]s in a [[legal]] [[contract]] that specify the {{l|en|price#Noun}} and also how and when {{l|en|payment#Noun}} must be made.
##: {{ux|en|Q: What are your company's '''terms'''? A: Net thirty, cash or check. [This answer means that the net total must be paid within 30 days; see ''{{w|Net D}}''.]}}
##: {{ux|en|The latest models are available now, on the lowest '''terms''' you'll find anywhere, guaranteed.}}
##* '''1793''' May 17, John Constable and James Piper, advertisement for a packet-boat between Chestertown and Baltimore, Chestertown, Maryland, [[:File:Packet_Schooner.jpg]]:
##*: The Cabin is large and commodious, well calculated for the Accommodation of Paſengers. Merchandiſe, Produce, &c. carried on the loweſt '''Terms'''.
# A chronological [[limitation]] or [[division]].
## {{senseid|en|limited timespan}} A limited or defined [[timespan]].
##: {{ux|en|The '''term''' of a lease agreement is the period of time during which the lease is effective, and may be fixed, periodic, or of indefinite duration.}}
##: {{ux|en|He was [[sentenced]] to a '''term''' of six years in [[prison]].}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''term''' allowed to a debtor to discharge his debt.}}
##: {{ux|en|near-'''term''', mid-'''term''' and long-'''term''' goals}}
## {{senseid|en|officeholding duration}} [[duration|Duration]] of officeholding, or its limit; period in office of fixed length.
##: {{ux|en|The president is almost half way through his '''term'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|The democrats are in for another '''term'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Observer|author=Ruth Michaelson|quotee=w:Ehud Barak|title=Netanyahu told to ‘quit now’ as ex-leaders pin blame on dysfunctional government|date=2023-10-22|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/22/netanyahu-told-to-quit-now-as-ex-leaders-pin-blame-on-dysfunctional-government|passage=“I don’t believe that the people trust Netanyahu to lead when he is under the burden of such a devastating event that just happened under his '''term''',” he told the ''Observer''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261011045726/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/oct/22/netanyahu-told-to-quit-now-as-ex-leaders-pin-blame-on-dysfunctional-government|archivedate=11 October 2026}}
##* {{quote-web|1=en|date=2026-07-27|author=Daniel Rugunya|quotee=w:Donald J. Trump|work=w:Yahoo|title=Donald Trump Jokes About ‘Running For A Fourth '''Term'''’ At Rescheduled WHCD|url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/donald-trump-jokes-running-fourth-214000080.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260808045903/https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/donald-trump-jokes-running-fourth-214000080.html|archivedate=8 August 2026|text="To show just how much I care about the press, and because I want to save your ratings, I'm pleased to announce my intention to run for a fourth '''term''' as President of the United States," Trump told the audience.|accessdate=8 August 2026}}
## The time during which [[legal]] [[court]]s are open.
## {{rfv-sense|en}} Certain days on which [[rent]] is paid.
## {{senseid|en|academic calendar division}} [[part|Part]] of a [[year]], especially one of the divisions of an [[academic year]].
##: {{ux|en|At our school, sports day was always held at the end of the summer '''term'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author={{w|Bill Oddie}}|title=Gone Birding|publisher=Methuen|location=London|page=45|passage=From 1960 to 1963 I spent my '''terms''' at Cambridge University but was back home for the vacs[.]}}# {{senseid|en|academic calendar division}} [[part|Part]] of a [[year]], especially one of the divisions of an [[academic year]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author={{w|Bill Oddie}}|title=Gone Birding|publisher=Methuen|location=London|page=45|passage=From 1960 to 1963 I spent my '''terms''' at Cambridge University but was back home for the vacs[.]}}
## {{senseid|en|end of gestation}} With respect to a [[pregnancy]], the usual [[duration]] of [[gestation]] for the given species (for example, nine months in humans); {{lb|en|metonymically}} the [[end#Noun|end]] of this duration: the [[timepoint]] at which [[birth]] usually happens (for example, in humans, approximately 40 weeks from [[conception]]), defining the [[due date]].
##: {{ux|en|A pregnancy didn't [[come to]] '''term'''.}}
##: {{coi|en|at '''term'''}}; &emsp; {{coi|en|[[preterm]]}}; &emsp; {{coi|en|[[postterm]]}}
## {{senseid|en|menstrual period, archaically}} {{lb|en|archaic}} A menstrual period.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1660|author=w:Samuel Pepys|title=Diary
|passage=My wife, after the absence of her '''terms''' for seven weeks, gave me hopes of her being with child, but on the last day of the year she hath them again.}}
## {{senseid|en|patent duration}} {{lb|en|of a patent}} The [[maximum]] [[period]] during which the [[patent]] can be [[maintain]]ed into [[force]].
# {{senseid|en|relations, interpersonally}} {{lb|en|in the plural|usually in phrases such as ''[[on good terms]]'' and similar}} [[relation|Relations]] among people.
#: {{ux|en|We are on friendly '''terms''' with each other.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XLIV|page=361|passage=Not unnaturally, “Auntie” took this communication in bad part.{{...}}Next day she{{...}}tried to recover her ward by the hair of the head. Then, thwarted, the wretched creature went to the police for help; she was versed in the law, and perhaps had spared no pains to keep on good '''terms''' with the local constabulary.}}
# A unit or component with a certain meaning.
## {{senseid|en|name for a concept}} A [[word]] or [[phrase]] (e.g., [[noun phrase]], [[verb phrase]], [[open compound]]), especially one from a [[specialise]]d area of knowledge; a [[name]] for a concept.
##: {{ux|en|"Algorithm" is a '''term''' used in computer science.}}
##: {{ux|en|The noun phrase "red blood cell", the acronym "RBC", and the word "erythrocyte" are synonymous '''terms'''.}}
## {{lb|en|in plural}} A stated kind of language or terminology.
##: {{ux|en|Could you explain that in layman's '''terms'''?}}
##: {{ux|en|I told her [[in no uncertain terms|in no uncertain '''terms''']] that it was none of her business.}}
## {{senseid|en|component in mathematics}} {{lb|en|mathematics}} Any [[value]] ([[variable]] or [[constant]]) or [[expression]] separated from another term by a space or an appropriate character, in an overall expression or [[table]].
##: {{ux|en|All the '''terms''' of this sum cancel out.}}
##: {{ux|en|One only '''term''' is odd {{nobr|in (&thinsp;12; 3; 4&thinsp;).}}}}
## {{senseid|en|component of a syllogism}} {{lb|en|logic}} The [[subject]] or the [[predicate]] of a [[proposition]]; one of the three component parts of a [[syllogism]], each one of which is used twice.
##* {{RQ:Hamilton Metaphysics and Logic
|passage=The subject and predicate of a proposition are, after Aristotle, together called its '''terms''' or extremes.}}
# {{senseid|en|astrological dignity}} {{lb|en|astrology}} An [[essential dignity]] in which [[unequal]] segments of every astrological [[sign]] have internal [[rulerships]] which affect the power and integrity of each planet in a [[natal chart]].
# {{senseid|en|geometric limit, archaically}} {{lb|en|geometry|archaic}} A [[point]], [[line]], or [[superficies]] that limits.
#: {{ux|en|A line is the '''term''' of a superficies, and a superficies is the '''term''' of a solid.}}
# {{senseid|en|statue type}} {{lb|en|arts}} A [[statue]] of the upper body, sometimes without the arms, ending in a [[pillar]] or pedestal. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=iii|page=54|passage=The pillers that haue bolſtered vp thoſe '''tearmes''',<br>Are falne in cluſters at my conquering feet.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1773|author=w:Joshua Reynolds|editors=w:John Ingamells; w:John Edgcumbe|title=The Letters of Sir Joshua Reynolds|publisher=Yale|year_published=2000|page=42
|passage=You have been already informed, I have no doubt, of the subject which we have chosen: the adorning a '''Term''' of Hymen with festoons of flowers.}}
# {{senseid|en|carved piece of a ship}} {{lb|en|nautical}} A piece of carved work placed under each end of the [[taffrail]].

